# One GPC's IPL, captioned -- ipl-one-gpc.script with the commentary a viewer
# needs.  Same parameters, same waits, same order:
#
#   script ipl-one-gpc-circled.script gpc=2 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2
#
#   gpc   the GPC column the O6 controls act on, 1-5
#   crt   the CRT its IPL display goes to, and the one waited on
#   idp   the IDP given the spring-loaded LOAD toggle
#   kb    the keyboard ITEM 1 EXEC is typed on: KB1, KB2 or KB3
#
# WHY A SEPARATE FILE rather than captions in the original: the original is
# played by every unattended run in the tree, where a caption is at best
# wasted work and at worst a window nobody asked for.  A video wants the
# talking; a measurement does not.  The two stay in step because this file is
# the other one with subtitle lines interleaved and NOTHING ELSE changed --
# if you alter one, alter both.
#
# The waits are the point and are kept verbatim.  Read ipl-one-gpc.script's
# own comments for why 'new-screen' has to come before 'title' (three
# computers share CRT2, and a stale GPCIPL MENU satisfied the title test
# instantly) and why the timeouts are sized for the slowest host we own.
# A caption budget must never become a guessed delay: every caption here sits
# between actions that are themselves gated on a wait.

+1   gpc $gpc
+2   source MM1
+2   crt $crt
+2   idpload $idp
+2   ipl
+0 circle activity-mm1 red 1.5
+2   nocircle
+1 mode STANDBY
+0 circle activity-mm1 red 1.5
# test alone is met by the PREVIOUS computer's menu; see ipl-one-gpc.script.
wait crt $crt new-screen timeout 120
+0 nocircle
wait crt $crt title GPCIPL timeout 120

+2   keys $kb ITEM 1 EXEC
+0 circle activity-mm1 red 1.5

# loaded: to RUN, and the IPL source back off
wait gpc $gpc mode-tb RUN timeout 120
+0 nocircle

+2   crt 0

+0 mode RUN
+0 circle outputtb1 red 1.5
+1   source OFF
+10 nocircle

+1   keys GPC/CRT $gpc $crt EXEC
+5   subtitle

